            <ArticleTitle>Multiple Neuromas Cause Painful &quot;Jumping Stump&quot; in a Transfemoral Amputee: A Case Report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>271-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1177/1534734616657964</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Painful &quot;jumping stump&quot; is an uncommon but very disturbing complication postamputation. This condition is one of the movement disorder entities resulting from peripheral nerve pathology, often known as &quot;peripherally induced movement disorders.&quot; Previously case reports have been written about painful and nonpainful incidence of &quot;jumping stump&quot;; however, only the earliest &quot;jumping stump&quot; article in 1852 suspected that neuromas might influence the involuntary movement. In this study, we describe a 38-year-old man with bilateral transfemoral amputee who suffered from painful &quot;jumping stump&quot; with multiple neuromas confirmed by imaging. He was treated successfully by ultrasound-guided phenol injection into the sciatic neuroma stalks. The pathophysiology of jumping stump and its possible association with neuroma are briefly discussed.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>© The Author(s) 2016.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
